title: Callaghan Decides
tags: event
view-if: time = 9 and election_called = 0
max-visits: 1
new-page: true
on-arrival: {!
// Callaghan goes if Labour is ahead; if the polls are level, it's a coin toss.
var labour_lead = Q.lab_votes - Q.con_votes;
Q.callaghan_calls = (labour_lead >= 1 || (labour_lead > -1 && Math.random() < 0.5)) ? 1 : 0;
!}
go-to: election if callaghan_calls = 1; no_election if callaghan_calls = 0

= Callaghan Decides

All summer, Westminster has assumed Callaghan will go to the country in October, before the 5% pay limit can unravel. At the TUC conference, all he offered was a music-hall song, "There was I, waiting at the church". Tonight he makes a television broadcast to the nation.

@election
on-arrival: autumn_election_1978 = 1; time_to_election = 1; callaghan_rating += 2
call: set_election

With the polls running his way, Callaghan seizes his moment. Parliament will be dissolved, and the country votes in [+ next_election_month : month +] [+ next_election_year +].

We have weeks, not months. Whatever we have built so far is what we fight with.

@no_election
on-arrival: callaghan_rating -= 2; labour_credibility -= 1; wet_dissent -= 2; loyalist_dissent -= 2

Callaghan says there will be no election this autumn. He has decided to see out the winter and go to the country in 1979.

Our candidates, already printing leaflets, are relieved and furious in equal measure. Callaghan has just bet his government on the 5% pay limit holding through the winter.
